Title: The Underground Railroad
Order: 4
Production Code: 1x04

INT. STEWARD'S CABIN - NIGHT

PAGE: 1

PANEL: A one-room cabin, midnight, rain on the shutters. A tallow candle on a stand; on the four-posted bed a woman and two small children asleep, and half-dressed on the edge of it PARSON STEWARD stretched out with his eyes open, listening.

CAPTION: There came a knock at Preacher Sampson Steward's cabin door that same night about midnight.

PANEL: Another knock. Steward springs to the floor and takes pistols from the stand, pushing them handy beside the candle; he seizes his rifle and cocks it.

SFX: KNOCK KNOCK

PANEL: Steward slips the heavy iron bolt with one hand, steps back a pace and draws a bead on the door, face set, lips tight.

PANEL: Close on Steward's eye along the barrel.

STEWARD
Come in!

PAGE: 2

PANEL: The door swings open on a gust of wind and rain. The tall, stout figure of MAYBEE is outlined against the black, mud to his knees, a driving whip in one hand, the muzzle of the rifle an inch from his chest. He does not blink.

MAYBEE
This ain't at all 'bligatory on you, Steward. You ought to know my knock by this time. Put up your gun.

PANEL: Steward stands the rifle against the wall and takes Maybee's hand, wry and tired.

STEWARD
Is it you, Maybee? Come in.

PANEL: Steward shows the window, the wooden shutter; he is lined with strain.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
God forgive me, Maybee, but it is more than human nature can stand.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
Sunday week it was only by a special act of Providence that my congregation escaped massacre.

PANEL: Steward drops his voice, a glance at the children.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
Since then I'm a marked man. I had a message from the Rangers.

PANEL: Maybee and Steward trade a long look.

MAYBEE
Oh. I see. Perhaps then we'd better bring in our fugitives at once.

STEWARD
What have you this time?

PAGE: 3

PANEL: Maybee, shrugging his shoulders, a thumb jerking at the dark.

MAYBEE
A young man and woman and a young Englishman, who is helping them away. It's a long story.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
All of 'em's good shots; the gal ain't slow on a pinch.

PANEL: Steward blows out the candle; the room is lit only by the stove's glow.

STEWARD
Good. The Lord sent you, Maybee. Now bring them in.

PANEL: The door opens and three figures slip in out of the dark and the rain: WARREN with the saddle-bags, JUDAH huge and silent, and WINONA in a wet cotton gown with her cropped hair plastered down.

PANEL: Steward looks at Winona by firelight with vague wonder.

CAPTION: In all his life he had seen no woman to compare with her.

PANEL: The fire in the stove, thrown with wood; the rain on the roof, all six faces lit orange.

PAGE: 4

PANEL: Steward at the stove frying strips of bacon, Maybee beside him slicing potatoes, Warren against the wall with his arm hanging, tired out. Winona seated on a stool, hands in her lap; Judah standing guard by the door.

STEWARD
Now, Maybee, where from and where bound? Tell me all about it.

PANEL: Warren to the preacher, curious.

WARREN
You have as many manoeuvres to gain admittance to your house as some of the Indian fighters I used to read about.

WARREN (CONT'D)
What are you expecting to-night, Mr. Steward?

PANEL: Steward, cutting bacon.

STEWARD
Some of the gang. They have threatened me with vengeance because I sheltered John Brown and his men on their way north.

PANEL: Steward lowers the knife.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
Reynolds brought me word this morning that they had concluded to visit me to-night.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
He thinks their intention is to decorate my anatomy with tar and feathers.

PANEL: Warren, quietly, with the weight of the question.

WARREN
Mr. Steward, if we had been of their number when we came to the door just now, what would you have done?

PAGE: 5

PANEL: Steward looks him straight in the eye with a half-peeled potato in one hand and the knife in the other.

STEWARD
Had you been one of Bill Thomson's riders, I would have sent a bullet through you without a word.

PANEL: Warren, perplexed; the preacher's Bible open on the table by the candle.

WARREN
I cannot understand how you can reconcile such a course with your profession.

WARREN (CONT'D)
I make no pretention to piety myself, but I have a profound respect for those who do.

PANEL: Maybee chuckles into his coffee.

MAYBEE
That's jest what I've wanted to say to Steward here, many a time, but I ain't gifted with gab,

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
which mos' people calls eddi-kation.

PANEL: Steward, lit up with holy anger, gesturing with a pistol toward the shutter.

STEWARD
What better course could I have pursued with the mob that came to our church during service?

STEWARD (CONT'D)
I girded up my loins and, taking a pistol in each hand, led forth my elders against the Philistines.

PANEL: Steward's face in the firelight, quieter, flat.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
Verily, not one was spared. To-night I was here single-handed. I have a wife and two children dependent on me for support.

PAGE: 6

PANEL: Warren, rising and pacing, a hand on his hurt shoulder.

WARREN
It is not your defense I question, but your aggressive spirit.

WARREN (CONT'D)
These men are a part of the territorial militia; do not your acts smack somewhat of treason?

PANEL: Steward slams the knife into the table.

STEWARD
Treason! The word by which traitors seek to hang those who resist them.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
Oppression is oppression, whether it enslaves men and women or shuts your mouth and mine.

PANEL: Steward, a finger to the ceiling.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
Three hundred will not excuse the number that have passed this station on the underground railroad since I have been here.

PANEL: Warren stops pacing, convinced.

WARREN
I concur with you. You have a perfect right to defend your home from brutal attack.

WARREN (CONT'D)
But let us hope the South will see its error and the Negroes will be granted freedom by peaceful means.

PANEL: Steward and Maybee laugh silently and heartily at the young man; Winona and Judah do not.

MAYBEE
Time 'n tide nor God A'mighty won't change the honery skunks. Them's my sentimen's.

PAGE: 7

PANEL: Judah, from the door, with wild exultation in his voice; everyone turns.

JUDAH
The storm is but gathering force.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
These bloody happenings are but the preliminary to a glorious struggle

JUDAH (CONT'D)
which will end in the breaking of every chain that binds human beings to servitude.

PANEL: Warren, astonished, a step toward him.

WARREN
Why do you think so, Judah?

PANEL: Judah, simply, a hand on the door.

JUDAH
I cannot tell.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
But I feel that the sin will be punished in a great outpouring of blood and treasure until God says it is enough.

PANEL: Steward, deeply moved, his hand raised.

STEWARD
Amen! The boy is a true prophet. Bring your chairs up to the table and have some hot coffee and a bit to eat.

PANEL: The five of them around the plain table, tin plates, coffee steaming, Steward with the bread, a plain pine table lit by one candle. Winona's face lifts to Warren's for the first time in the scene.

PAGE: 8

PANEL: Steward at a ladder, throwing back a trap door in the ceiling hidden by festoons of dried apples and bunches of onions and herbs; he beckons to Winona to climb.

STEWARD
Speak softly. Go up, child, and I'll take the ladder away behind you.

PANEL: Winona climbs the rungs, one hand on the festoons, a candle in the other; Warren watching from below with a pistol in his belt.

PANEL: Steward drags wooden sea chests out from beneath the tall bed where his wife and children are asleep and spreads a pair of blankets at the extreme back.

PANEL: Judah creeps under the bed on his belly; the chests are pushed back to their old positions behind him until he is completely hidden.

PANEL: The men lie down on a buffalo robe on the floor, boots off, weapons within reach. Steward blows out the light.

MAYBEE
It's the best the railroad can offer under the circumstances. The railroad isn't wealthy and we have to put up with some discomforts.

PAGE: 9

PANEL: Dark. Warren on his back, eyes open, listening to the rain; a clock ticks on the stand. An hour passes. A footstep outside.

PANEL: A hand passed cautiously over the door from outside, a dark shape under the crack of it.

PANEL: Warren raises himself on one elbow, a pistol up; he touches Steward's arm.

STEWARD
Who's there?

OUTSIDE (O.P.)
Travellers; we want to find the road.

PANEL: Maybee jumps to his feet.

MAYBEE
Where yer boun?

OUTSIDE (O.P.)
Nebraska.

PANEL: Steward at the door in his shirt, not touching the bolt; Maybee whispering behind him. Their gun barrels point at the door.

STEWARD
You're right for that. This is the Jim Lane route. Keep the main road and you'll not miss it.

PAGE: 10 (TURN)

PANEL: The door in the dark; the two men listening at it.

OUTSIDE (O.P.)
Can you put us up till mornin'?

MAYBEE
Cayn't do it. Our beds are full. How many of you?

OUTSIDE (O.P.)
Two. Say, have you seen anything of a nigger man an' gal an' a white man a-pilotin' 'em?

PANEL: Close on Maybee's face a half inch from the wood; not a flicker.

MAYBEE
Nary one, mister.

PANEL: A last panel: the sound of horses' feet dying away; Warren with the pistol, lowering it slowly.

OUTSIDE (O.P.)
Reckon we'll push on then.

SFX: clop-clop-clop

PAGE: 11

EXT. THE WOODS - MORNING

PANEL: Dawn through wet trees. Steward at the head of a small cavalcade, Maybee at the rear; Winona between Warren and Judah. Warren has just helped her to mount, a hand under her boot.

CAPTION: In the early morning light they rode away through the quiet beauty of the woods.

PANEL: Winona on horseback, light on her face, holding the reins with no uncertain hand; Warren riding at her side. Her thoughts in a caption.

CAPTION: Winona was only sixteen, and she was dreaming the first enchanted dream of youth.

PANEL: Close on her, in profile, serious; flowers behind her, a bird in flight.

CAPTION: One thing was certain: she would never, never marry.

PANEL: Warren glances at her; she turns; their eyes meet; both look away at once.

PANEL: Judah, a step behind, his hand tight on the reins, his face hardening.

JUDAH
The white man has the advantage in all things.

PAGE: 12

PANEL: Judah rides with his head bowed, shoulders in shadow.

CAPTION: Was it for this he had suffered and toiled to escape from his bonds?

PANEL: Judah, lifting his eyes again, hard and fierce.

CAPTION: Let Maxwell beware, and let it end in admiration only.

PANEL: Close on Judah's profile: a settling.

CAPTION: He was her natural protector; he would be faithful to White Eagle's trust.

PANEL: Toward twilight. The party leaves the road and enters a wooded slope in a basin of hills; ribbed rocks, a creek, smoke from a fire.

PANEL: A shot rings out from a rock above; the riders halt. Two young men with guns step from the bushes.

PICKET
Stop thar. Free or pro-slavery? Whar you from?

PAGE: 13

EXT. BROWN'S CAMP - TWILIGHT

PANEL: The camp in the cup of the hills: a blazing fire, a pot over it, a sunburned woman stirring; saddled horses; three or four armed men on blankets; two youths leaning on their rifles.

PANEL: Old John Brown himself at the fire, shirt-sleeves rolled up, a large piece of pork in his hands cut from a pig barely cold. Lean, upright, gray.

PANEL: A group of women surrounds Winona and hurries her to the largest of the huts; she looks back over her shoulder at the men.

PANEL: Rude shelter-huts of tree branches nestle in the dark woods behind, firelight dancing on their walls.

PANEL: Morning. Warren stands with Winona by the creek, saddled horse behind him; she is pale with the smallest tremor in her lip.

WARREN
Good-bye, Winona; I shall return in a few weeks at longest. You are safe now until we can reach Canada.

PAGE: 14

PANEL: Winona, clear-eyed, her hands at her sides.

WINONA
Good-bye, Mr. Maxwell. Do not speak so confidently.

WINONA (CONT'D)
How can we tell that you will ever return or that I shall ever see Canada? I hate these good-byes.

PANEL: Warren takes her childish hand in his and kisses it.

WARREN
Let us add "God willing."

PANEL: Steward, already mounted, calls from the trail.

STEWARD
No more time. We've a good twenty miles and a bit before night.

PANEL: Two men shake hands with Maybee and Judah; riding out of camp. Winona stands at the creek, one hand raised, the other at her throat.

PANEL: Wide in a green wood: Steward riding ahead, Warren behind, evening gold. Steward is singing a hymn in a good baritone.

CAPTION: A charge to keep I have, a God to glorify; a never-dying soul to save, and fit it for the skies.

PAGE: 15

PANEL: They ride through the evening past a burned house, black timbers against the sky, a chimney standing, fence rails scattered in the weeds.

CAPTION: The ghostly ruins of charred houses lifted their scarred skeletons against the sky in a mute appeal for vengeance.

PANEL: Steward singing again, his face lifted, eyes half closed; Warren dreaming beside him of an English avenue of trees and a manse in the dusk, faces smiling from the bushes.

CAPTION: Praise God from whom all blessings flow.

PANEL: Steward ahead of Warren, suddenly halting his horse with one hand up.

STEWARD
Ssh!

PANEL: Both men sit motionless in the twilight. A soft clink of metal against stone, though no one is visible among the ghostly trees. Steward peers about like a fox.

SFX: clink

PANEL: Warren's horse trembles under him; his hand closes on the pommel.

PAGE: 16

PANEL: Muzzle flash from the underbrush, a flash of white.

SFX: KRAK

PANEL: Warren pitches forward in the saddle; his arm drops useless, his face white.

PANEL: Another flash and Steward's cry is bigger than the shot.

STEWARD
Blow ye the trumpet blow! Slay and spare not!

SFX: KRAK KRAK

PANEL: A riderless horse bolts through the trees with Warren hanging in the saddle, galloping blind through the dusk.

PANEL: Half a mile on, the horse stumbles over an obstacle in the path and Warren is thrown clear, lying on his back in the leaves under the first stars.

CAPTION: Warren lay motionless under the blossoming stars.

PAGE: 17

PANEL: Lantern light and boots. A ring of shadowy men with a torch standing over him, Thomson in front, bending to feel his heart.

THOMSON
Hold the light. He ain't dead, is he?

PANEL: GIDEON HOLMES, lean, long-fingered, Thomson's lieutenant, at his shoulder.

HOLMES
Looks like it, but reckon he's only wounded.

PANEL: Thomson takes the money-belt off Warren's waist and divides the contents among his followers like a man dealing cards; one has the boots, one the coat, one the hat, one the saddle-bags.

CAPTION: It was a motley crowd of Southern desperadoes, men who stopped at nothing in the line of murder and rapine.

PANEL: Two Rangers whisper at the back of the group.

DAN
Stole two o' his niggers, so he says.

JIM
Nasty mess. He won't git off easy.

PANEL: Warren's eyes open; Thomson's devilish face above him in the torchlight.

THOMSON
Howd'y, Mr. Maxwell? Didn't think I'd meet up with you so soon again, did you?

PAGE: 18

PANEL: Thomson, hands on knees, leaning over him, friendly as a snake.

THOMSON
Been after you ever since you left the "Crescent." Now, I want my niggers. I ain't foolin'. Where's they at?

WARREN
I can't tell you.

PANEL: Thomson, soft now, the voice of a man making an offer.

THOMSON
You answer me true an' straight an' I'll make it all right for you. If you don't, I'll let a Missouri crowd kill you.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
It won't be nice, easy killin', neither.

PANEL: Warren in the lantern light, cold drops at the roots of his hair, looks up resolutely.

WARREN
I can't tell you.

PANEL: Close on Warren's eyes; a faint figure of Winona behind them, a ghost over the trees.

WINONA (V.O.)
Be true.

PANEL: Warren answers with sudden boldness.

WARREN
You have no right to claim either Winona or Judah as your slave. They are as free as you or I.

WARREN (CONT'D)
I will never aid and abet your barbarous system.

PAGE: 19

PANEL: A cry from the crowd; Thomson waves them back, grinning.

THOMSON
Let him free his mind.

PANEL: Holmes, leaning in with a lean grin; Warren steady.

HOLMES
We take the law into our own hands these times.

WARREN
I have committed no crime against your laws; if so, why, leave me in the hands of the law.

PANEL: Two riders emerge from the woods leading Steward's horse; the pistols of the "fighting parson" dangle from the saddle of the man who leads.

PANEL: The man who leads the horse, nodding at Thomson's question.

THOMSON
Where's he at?

RANGER
Dead's a hammer.

PANEL: Close on Warren's face as the horse is led past; he turns his head away.

PAGE: 20

PANEL: Thomson stands over Warren with his hands on his hips.

THOMSON
Git anything out of him about my niggers?

RANGER
No use, Bill; they're up to Brown's camp. Nex' week they'll be in Canidy.

PANEL: Thomson stares down at Warren, a great oath in his mouth.

THOMSON
Well, this one won't escape.

PANEL: Warren, lifted to the parson's horse and firmly bound, slumped, bleeding at the shoulder.

PANEL: A long wild night ride: horses plunging through bog, stumbling over stones, crossing a stream; Warren half-delirious, strapped to the saddle.

CAPTION: Bound, helpless, stabbed by every stumble.

PANEL: Two Rangers, riding on either side of him, talk low.

SAM
Fifteen hundred dollars for the slaves or the slave-stealer, dead or alive, that's what the Colonel has advertised.

DAN
A right smart o' money.

PAGE: 21

EXT. THE FERRY AT WESTON - NIGHT

PANEL: The river at the edge of the dark; a ferry crossing in torchlight, crowded with men in hats and boots. The Rangers push Warren onto the boat with a rifle butt.

CAPTION: At Weston a crowd of men awaited them.

PANEL: Warren thrown into a wagon on the Missouri bank, torchlight on the gold of his head, face to the sky.

PANEL: Torches thicken. A river of faces, hats, hands, rope.

PANEL: Close on a rope coiled over a man's shoulder.

PANEL: Close on Warren's eyes, wide open.

CAPTION: Presently they stepped from the boat to Missouri's shore.
